A program whose sources lie in directories of one String.hashCode, for the native link's tests (tests/native-link.scala).
Scala Native's code generator writes a module for each directory of sources and names its file after the directory's
hash (generated/<hash>.ll, the hexadecimal String.hashCode): AaAa, AaBB and BBBB have one hash (1f0080, as Aa and BB
have one), so its own build writes their three modules into one file. teq names the module of a directory as Scala
Native does where no other directory of the program has its hash; of directories of one hash, in their bytes' order,
the first keeps it and the next are <hash>-1, <hash>-2, ... (src/native/codegen.rs, `module_names`).

src/          the program: the objects A (AaAa/A.scala), B (AaBB/B.scala) and C (BBBB/C.scala), whose values Main
              (main/Main.scala) prints; scalac 3.8.4's JVM build of it prints 820959446. project.scala holds the build's
              directives
nir/          the .nir files of the program, written by the compiler plugin of Scala Native 0.5.12 for Scala 3.8.4
              (scala-cli compile of src/, the files taken from the classes directory); each position names its source
              by its path under src/ (AaAa/A.scala, ...)
modules.txt   the program's modules: of each, the module's file, the directory its sources lie in and the classes of
              those sources (the .nir files whose positions name the directory), the file named by the rule above
digests.txt   the SHA-256 and the lines of what Scala Native's own tools leave for the program, linked from nir/ and the
              jars of tests/nir/digests.txt as tests/native-link/README says for its program, once as the build decides
              on threads and once linked with them: its link's dump (linked), its lowered definitions (lowered) and each
              module of the LLVM IR its code generator writes but __buildInfo.ll (ll), the three of one hash as below
dirs.Main.txt of the first lowered dump, the definitions of the program's own classes, by the rule of
              tests/native-link.scala's definitionsOf: what a failure is read against

Scala Native's own build is no oracle of the three modules of one hash. Each of its writers of a module writes
<hash>-body.ll, <hash>-metadata.ll and <hash>.ll and then appends the first two to the last, deleting them, so the
writers of one hash run into each other: of five runs of its tools over nir/, four failed in the code generation
(java.nio.file.NoSuchFileException: generated/1f0080-body.ll) and one ended with 1f0080.ll holding B's module alone,
A's and C's lost. Their expectations are therefore Scala Native's modules of the same sources compiled under the
directories AaAa0, AaBB1 and BBBB2 instead (in the same order, each of a hash of its own: 3c10fb0, 3c10fb1 and 3c10fb2),
each recorded under the name teq gives its directory here (1f0080.ll, 1f0080-1.ll and 1f0080-2.ll). The directory
enters nothing else with the debug metadata off: the .nir files of the two compilations differ in their positions'
text alone; Scala Native's link and lowering of the two are the same bytes in every run; and in the one run over nir/
that ended (threads as the build decides; none with threads ended), main's module and every module of the jars are
those of the second compilation byte for byte, and the one module it kept, 1f0080.ll, is that of AaBB1 (3c10fb1.ll).
The linked and lowered digests are those of the runs over nir/ (both are dumped before the modules are written); two
runs of the tools over the second compilation left the same bytes.

To make it again: scala-cli compile src/ and take the .nir files of its classes directory into nir/dirs; copy src/ with
AaAa, AaBB and BBBB renamed AaAa0, AaBB1 and BBBB2 (main kept) and compile that the same way; run Scala Native's tools
over each, as tests/native-link/README says; take linked and lowered from the first, ll from the second with the three
renamed by modules.txt. No file here is written from teq's output.
